* The schedule editor

** The grid
#+INDEX: schedule grid
#+INDEX: combo box delegate

The grid is a =QTableView= over =models/table_model.py=. Columns are the days
found in the header row, so a six-day table and a seven-day table both work
without a setting. Rows follow the file order, which keeps section rows in
place. A section row renders as a bold spanning row and refuses edits. A
time-block row renders one cell per day, and each cell uses a
=QStyledItemDelegate= that presents a combo box holding the legend codes plus a
blank entry, so a cell can never hold a code the legend does not define.

Three edits change the shape of the week rather than one cell. Adding a time
block inserts a row under the current section and asks for the time range with
a validated line edit. Adding a section appends a section row and one block
row. Adding a day is out of scope for the first release, because the day set
comes from the header row and the parser reads it once.

** The live readouts
#+INDEX: live validation
#+INDEX: overlap warning

Every edit triggers one revalidation pass on the in-memory table, which is
cheap because a week holds a few dozen blocks. The pass produces four
readouts. The overlap panel lists the clashes from
=writing_schedule.overlap.find_overlaps=, and the offending cells take a warning
background, which is the graphical equivalent of the =check= subcommand and of
the =--strict= guard. The totals panel shows planned minutes per day, per
project, and per activity class. The code readout shows the canonical file-name
code for the current grid. The legend check panel shows the output of
=name.check_against_legend=, so an unknown or ambiguous project letter is
visible before the file is saved.

** The encoder
#+INDEX: name encoder
#+INDEX: canonical file name

=name.py= decoded a code into a week and had no inverse, so the editor had no
way to name the file it saves. =name.encode(week)= and =name.encode_day(blocks)=
now supply that inverse. They live in the library rather than in the graphical
layer, because the command line and the Emacs Lisp twin want the same function.

The canonical spelling follows =docs/table-file-naming-rules.org=. A maximal run
of identical consecutive days collapses into one group carrying the day count, a
count of one is omitted, trailing open days are dropped because they are
implied, and an interior open day is written as =o=. Two days that share a
pattern without being adjacent are written out again, which is the rule you
chose, so a Monday, Wednesday, Friday week reads =gA-o-gA-o-gA=. The property
=encode(decode(code)) == code= holds for every canonical code, and the corpus
test asserts the weaker semantic property =decode(encode(decode(name))) ==
decode(name)= for every file name in =templates/=.

One ambiguity surfaced when the encoder ran over that corpus, and it needs a
decision before the editor renames anything. Ten of the twelve template names
merge a run of blocks that share an activity, so three generative blocks read
=gAAB=. Two names instead repeat the activity letter at each change of project,
so =sBBBsCCCsD= carries three support runs where the merged spelling is
=sBBBCCCD=. Both forms decode to the same week, and the six canonical rules do
not choose between them. The encoder merges, because merging is the shorter form
and it matches ten of the twelve names. The consequence is that
=2gAsBBBsCCCsD-2gAsBBBsCCCsE-gWsBBBsCCC-sDDDsEEE.org= and
=4gAsBBBsCCC-sBBBsCCC.org= are not canonical under that rule and would be
renamed. Confirm the rule, then rename those two files, or state the opposite
rule and the encoder changes in one place.

** Saving
#+INDEX: file naming
#+INDEX: round trip

Saving offers the canonical name =<code>.org= in the template directory and
allows an override. The writer emits the same four row kinds in the same order,
with column widths recomputed, so a file that was not edited round-trips to
identical bytes. That property is the first test to write, because it is the
one failure a user cannot recover from.

* The command panel

** Generating forms from argparse
#+INDEX: argparse introspection
#+INDEX: generated forms

=argspec.py= walks a parser and yields a descriptor for every subcommand and
every option. The walk reads =parser._subparsers=, then for each subparser reads
=_actions=, skipping the help action. Each action gives the option strings, the
=dest=, the =help= text, the =choices=, the =type=, the =required= flag, and
whether it is a =store_true= action. Private attributes are involved, so the
walk is confined to =argspec.py= and is covered by a test that fails loudly when
a future Python changes the layout.

#+CAPTION: The rule that maps an argparse action to a widget.
#+ATTR_LATEX: :booktabs t :center t :environment tabularx :width \maxtablewidth :align LLL
| Action shape | Widget | Example |
|--------------+--------+---------|
| =action="store_true"= | =QCheckBox= | =--strict=, =--no-ics=, =--per-day= |
| =choices= present | =QComboBox= | =--format=, =--engine= |
| =type=int= | =QSpinBox= | =--minutes= |
| =dest= in the date set | =QDateEdit= with a calendar popup and a "today" button | =--week=, =--day=, =--from=, =--to= |
| =dest= naming a directory | line edit plus a directory chooser | =--dir= |
| =dest= naming a file or a positional path | line edit plus a file chooser with a suffix filter | =--out=, =--plot=, =--table=, =path= |
| anything else | =QLineEdit= | =--tag=, =--note=, =--tz= |

The date set and the path set are two small tables in =forms.py=, keyed by
=dest=. A new option that falls outside both still produces a working line edit,
so an unrecognized flag degrades to a usable widget rather than to a crash.

** Running a command
#+INDEX: worker thread
#+INDEX: sqlite thread safety

The run button collects the widget values into an argument list, renders the
preview string, and hands a callable to =runner.py=. The worker runs on a
=QThread= and communicates only through signals, because touching a widget from
a worker thread is the standard way to crash a Qt program. The worker
redirects =sys.stdout= and =sys.stderr= with =contextlib.redirect_stdout= so that
the printed output of the existing functions reaches the log pane unchanged.

The database needs care. =sqlite3= connections belong to the thread that
created them, so the worker calls =db.connect= itself and closes the connection
when the task ends. The main thread keeps no long-lived connection. Read-only
panels that need a quick query open a short connection on the main thread and
close it immediately, which is acceptable at this data volume.

** Preferences and defaults
#+INDEX: QSettings
#+INDEX: default paths

The database path, the template directory, the output directory, the time zone,
and the last used week persist through =QSettings=. Every form seeds its widgets
from those settings, so the common case of running =plan import=, then
=compare=, then =dashboard= for the same week needs no retyping.

The =--db= widget stays on every command form, which is your decision and which
matches the command line, where =--db= is required on every subcommand that
touches the database. Two habits keep a per-command path from becoming a source
of silent mistakes. The remembered path seeds every form, so the widgets agree
unless you deliberately change one. The status bar shows the path of the
database the last command actually used, together with its row counts, so a
command that ran against the wrong file is visible immediately rather than three
commands later.

* Packaging

** The optional extra
#+INDEX: optional dependency
#+INDEX: entry point

The graphical layer ships inside =writing-habit= behind an extra, so the wheel
and the command line stay free of a Qt dependency.

#+begin_src toml
[project.optional-dependencies]
gui = ["PyQt5>=5.15", "writing-schedule>=0.3.1"]

[project.scripts]
writing-habit = "writing_habit.cli:main"
writing-habit-gui = "writing_habit.gui.app:main"
#+end_src

Three rules keep the extra honest. No module outside =writing_habit/gui/= may
import Qt. The =gui= package imports Qt only through =gui/qt.py=. The
=writing-habit-gui= entry point catches the import error and prints the
installation hint =pip install "writing-habit[gui]"= rather than a traceback.

The =gui= extra depends on =writing-schedule= because the editor uses its parser,
its overlap detector, and its template scaffold. That package is already an
optional dependency under the =plan= extra, so the two extras should be
documented together.

