* Risks and open questions
#+INDEX: risks

#+CAPTION: The risks that deserve a decision before coding, and the response to each.
#+ATTR_LATEX: :booktabs t :center t :environment tabularx :width \maxtablewidth :align LLL
| Risk | Why it matters | Response |
|------+----------------+----------|
| Private =argparse= attributes | =_actions= and =_subparsers= are not public API | confine the access to =argspec.py= and test it explicitly |
| The repeated activity letter | =sBBBsCCCsD= and =sBBBCCCD= decode alike, and the six canonical rules do not choose | the encoder merges; confirm the rule and rename the two template files |
| Byte-exact round trip | column padding and trailing spaces vary between hand-edited files | normalize on load, and warn the user when the first save will reformat |
| GPL of PyQt5 beside MIT | a split license needs a plain statement | state it in the README, the =About= dialog, and the installation page |
| Threading with sqlite3 | a connection shared across threads raises at runtime | open and close the connection inside the worker |
| A per-command database path | one form can point at a different file than the last | seed every form from the remembered path, and name the database of the last run in the status bar |
| Feature drift from the command line | a graphical action with no command-line equivalent splits the two ports | require a subcommand for every action, which step 0 restored for the dashboard |
| The Emacs Lisp twin | the elisp package has no matching interface, and it needs the same encoder | treat the graphical layer as Python-only, and port =encode= to the elisp package |
| Rounding in the golden fixtures | =tests/test_seasons.py= already fails because =ROUND= in SQL rounds 0.425 differently between SQLite builds | move the rounding into Python with the round-half-up rule the dashboard uses |

